 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity**: $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of voxels $(R-1)^3$ [G]. Each voxel is processed in parallel by a thread group [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity**: $O(N)$ for the append buffer, with capacity $(R-1)^3 \times 5$ triangles [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance**: Parallelizes the extraction process, significantly faster than CPU-based methods for high resolutions [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths**: Dynamic output size handled via append buffers and indirect draw calls; leverages GPU parallelism [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations**: Requires DX11; CPU density generation is a bottleneck in the reference implementation; potential topological artifacts in ambiguous cases [S1, unofficial][G].
+*   **Time Complexity**: $O(N^3)$ for a grid of size $N$, as each voxel is processed by a thread.
+*   **Memory Complexity**: $O(N^3)$ for the input texture and output buffer capacity. The output buffer is pre-allocated to the worst-case scenario (5 triangles per voxel) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Performance**: Performance is limited by the density field generation on the CPU in the provided example. GPU compute performance is generally high for dense volumes but depends on the complexity of the normal calculation and texture sampling [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths**: Simple implementation, leverages GPU parallelism, suitable for real-time rendering in Unity [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations**: CPU-bound density generation in the example, potential for high memory usage due to pre-allocated append buffer, limited to DX11 hardware [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use**: Real-time isosurface extraction in Unity for dense volumes where CPU generation is acceptable or replaced by GPU-generated textures.
+*   **When Not to Use**: Sparse volumes where memory efficiency is critical (use Histogram Pyramid), or non-DX11 hardware.
 
 ## Background
-This implementation is an unofficial GPU-based Marching Cubes algorithm for Unity, developed by Pavel Kouril [S1, unofficial]. It builds on the algorithm and lookup tables by Paul Bourke and uses gradient normals calculation methods from angelfire.com [S1, unofficial]. It was developed with Unity 5.6 and requires DX11 hardware [S1, unofficial].
+This implementation was developed for Unity 5.6 by Pavel Kouril [S1, unofficial]. It builds upon the original Marching Cubes algorithm and lookup tables by Paul Bourke [S1, unofficial]. The gradient normal calculation method is attributed to an improved MC implementation [S1, unofficial]. Debugging and troubleshooting assistance was provided by Vilém Otte [S1, unofficial].
 
